### What did Java 9 add to Optional?

Three methods improve Optional's integration with streams and fallback handling.

### How does Optional.stream() work?

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Optional;

public class AmountParser {

    // BEFORE Java 9: verbose
    List<BigDecimal> parseAll(List<String> inputs) {
        return inputs.stream()
            .map(this::tryParse)
            .filter(Optional::isPresent)
            .map(Optional::get)  // unsafe .get()!
            .toList();
    }

    // AFTER Java 9: clean
    List<BigDecimal> parseAllClean(List<String> inputs) {
        return inputs.stream()
            .map(this::tryParse)
            .flatMap(Optional::stream)  // present → value, empty → gone
            .toList();
    }

    private Optional<BigDecimal> tryParse(String input) {
        try {
            return Optional.of(new BigDecimal(input));
        } catch (NumberFormatException e) {
            return Optional.empty();
        }
    }
}
```

### How does Optional.or() work?

```java
// Try primary source. If empty, try secondary. If empty, try tertiary.
Optional<Account> account = findInSavings(accountId)
    .or(() -> findInCurrent(accountId))    // lazy: only called if savings is empty
    .or(() -> findInNRI(accountId));        // lazy: only called if current is also empty

// Key: or() returns Optional, enabling further chaining
// orElse() returns the VALUE, ending the chain
Account value = findInSavings(id).orElse(defaultAccount);  // returns Account
Optional<Account> opt = findInSavings(id).or(() -> findInCurrent(id));  // returns Optional
```

### How does Optional.ifPresentOrElse() work?

```java
// BEFORE: if-else
Optional<Account> account = findAccount(id);
if (account.isPresent()) {
    processAccount(account.get());
} else {
    log.warn("Account {} not found", id);
}

// AFTER: one method call
findAccount(id).ifPresentOrElse(
    this::processAccount,
    () -> log.warn("Account {} not found", id));
```

### What is the difference between or, orElse, and orElseGet?

| Method | Returns | Evaluation | Purpose |
|---|---|---|---|
| orElse(value) | T | Eager (always evaluated) | Default value |
| orElseGet(supplier) | T | Lazy (only if empty) | Computed default |
| or(supplier) | Optional<T> | Lazy (only if empty) | Fallback Optional chain |

### What should a senior engineer know?

- stream() bridges Optional to Stream: the pattern `flatMap(Optional::stream)` is the idiomatic way to drop empties.
- or() suppliers should be side-effect-free; they may or may not execute depending on whether earlier Optionals are present.
- ifPresentOrElse returns void; it is for side effects only, not for computing values.

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- Why is flatMap(Optional::stream) better than filter+get? (No unsafe .get() call. One method reference instead of two lines.)
- Can you chain or() multiple times? (Yes. Each supplier is tried in order until one returns a present Optional.)
- Does ifPresentOrElse return a value? (No. It returns void. For computed values, use map or flatMap.)
